# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for G-TSAN (.port/tools/gates/sanitizers.sh
# --gate tsan; run_crate passes it through TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=...:
# print_suppressions=1, so every match is listed in the crate log).
#
# Same rules as lsan.supp: every entry is a THIRD-PARTY race (a system
# framework or vendored C/C++), each with a row in
# .port/phase-2/SANITIZER_SUPPRESSIONS.md (ThreadSanitizer section, T ids)
# giving the reason and evidence, and a WHY comment directly above it (D6 rule
# c). A race in Rustty code is never suppressed: it is a finding (an N id, and
# a UD when Ghostty has it too), fixed with a regression test.
#
# G-TSAN's first runs (P7-W17, macOS; P7-W24, Linux) needed no entry. The
# first Linux run's rustty-xev reports (TSan's fd model, NEW-FINDINGS N514)
# are handled by running that crate's tests one at a time (TSAN_SERIAL in
# sanitizers.sh), not by a suppression.

# WHY (T5, NEW-FINDINGS N622 item 2): Mesa's EGL (system, uninstrumented).
# Two threads in eglGetPlatformDisplay: _eglFindDisplay (egldisplay.c:265,
# _eglSameAttribs's memcmp) reads the attrib copy another thread's
# _eglFindDisplay calloc'd (egldisplay.c:281). Both run under
# _eglGlobal.Mutex, a futex-based simple_mtx inside Mesa that TSan cannot see.
# EGL 1.5 section 2.6: "EGL and its client APIs must be threadsafe."
race:libEGL_mesa.so
# WHY (T6, NEW-FINDINGS N622 item 3): Mesa llvmpipe (system, uninstrumented).
# A context thread drops the last reference to an lp_fence and destroys its
# mutex and condvar (lp_fence_destroy, lp_fence.c:130-131) after one of the
# screen-wide rasterizer threads ("llvmpipe-N", lp_rast.c) signalled it under
# that mutex (lp_fence_signal). The order between the two is Mesa's atomic
# reference count (pipe_reference, u_inlines.h:88), which TSan cannot see.
race:libgallium
